<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vasto</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Histonium</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vasto is a coastal town in the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chieti">Chieti</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, on the Adriatic. As Histonium it was a centre of the Frentani and then a Roman municipium. In the early modern period it was held by the d'Avalos family, marquesses of Vasto, and its old town overlooks the Gulf of Vasto.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasto.jpg" alt="Vasto" width="320" height="476" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Luca from Vasto, Roma, Italia · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Il_Faro_di_Punta_Penna-2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>According to legend the town was founded by the Greek hero <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diomedes">Diomedes</a>, and its earliest archaeological relics date to 1300 BCE. Histonium, one of the key towns of the Frentani, did not obtain the rank of colonia under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> but kept the title of municipium. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> it was flourishing and opulent, as shown by the ruins of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, baths, other public buildings, mosaics and statues. An inscription records that a thirteen-year-old boy, Lucius Valerius Pudens, won the prize for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> poetry at a contest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The town was ravaged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a>, but probably never ceased to exist on its present site.</p><p>After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a> it fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> and joined the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>. About 774 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> conquered it, and in 1053 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-guiscard">Robert Guiscard</a> captured it. About 1076, after raids, it was renamed Guastaymonis, the Waste of Aimone. From the 13th century it belonged to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. In the 15th century the condottiero Giacomo Caldora, its lord, transformed its urban structure, and his family built the city walls still seen today and the Castello Caldoresco.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1566 Ottoman naval forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piyale-pasha">Piyale Pasha</a> destroyed much of the city by fire, including the Castello Caldoresco and the Palazzo d'Avalos. Under Spanish rule Vasto became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a> of the Marquis d'Avalos, and it reached its height under Cesare Michelangelo, marquis from 1697 to 1729. A short-lived Republic of Vasto in 1799 was quickly overthrown by loyalists. The poet and scholar Gabriele Rossetti was born there on 28 February 1783; exiled in 1821, he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and fathered Dante Gabriel and Christina Rossetti. Fascist Italy renamed the town Istonio in 1938, and it became Vasto again in 1944.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Vasto is a comune on the Adriatic coast of the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chieti">Chieti</a>, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abruzzo">Abruzzo</a>. Its old town has buildings and churches from the 12th to the 18th centuries, among them the Cathedral of San Giuseppe, the Castello Caldoresco and the Palazzo d'Avalos, which houses the Civic Museums. The church of Santa Maria Maggiore, mentioned in a document as early as 1195, was restructured in 1735. Below the hill lies the beach resort of Marina di Vasto, and the coast to the north has typical wooden fishing machines called trabocchi.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vasto” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Led by the Marquis del <b>Vasto</b>, a Spanish force broke through an English and Dutch ambush near Warnsveld and Zutphen, which the Earl of Leicester was besieging.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-22">22 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Their main centres included <b>Histonium</b> (Vasto), Anxanum (Lanciano) and Larinum, which some writers treated as a separate people.</blockquote><a href="/state/frentani">Frentani</a></li></ul></section>
